<SECTION SCID = "T060004A453S"><SCTITLE/>

<PARA INFOID = "T060004A453" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "28Jan2004"     INFOTYPE = "PARTS" INDSW = "ON">
<TITLE>Brake Master Cylinder &amp; Reservoir</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>A tandem type master cylinder is used. In addition, the master cylinder is inserted into the booster when mounted, shortening the mounting length.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>The reservoir tank is integrated with the master cylinder to save space. In addition, a level warning switch is provided to detect the brake fluid level.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740026c" WIDTH = "6.903in" HEIGHT = "3.944in" />
</FIGURE>
</PARA>
</SECTION>
<SECTION SCID = "T060004A219S"><SCTITLE/>

<PARA INFOID = "T060004A219" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "12Sep2003"     INFOTYPE = "PARTS" INDSW = "ON">
<TITLE>Brake Booster</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>A 10" single type brake booster is used on all models.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>Tie rods are provided inside the booster to ensure rigidity during booster operation, allowing the booster body plate thickness to be reduced for weight reduction.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740027c" WIDTH = "6.917in" HEIGHT = "5.264in" />
</FIGURE>
</PARA>
</SECTION>
<SECTION SCID = "T060004A221S"><SCTITLE/>

<PARA INFOID = "T060004A221" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "12Sep2003"     INFOTYPE = "PARTS" INDSW = "ON">
<TITLE>Front Brake</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>16" ventilated disc rotors and PE63 type calipers are used on models with the 2GR-FE engine.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740207c" WIDTH = "6.903in" HEIGHT = "3.597in" />
</FIGURE>
</PARA>
</SECTION>
<SECTION SCID = "T060004A222S"><SCTITLE/>

<PARA INFOID = "T060004A222" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "12Sep2003"     INFOTYPE = "PARTS" INDSW = "ON">
<TITLE>Rear Brake</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>All models use disc brakes with CLA11H type calipers and a rotor size of ø279mm × 10mm thickness. The calipers are a built-in type that also serves as the parking brake.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740166c" WIDTH = "6.903in" HEIGHT = "3.028in" />
</FIGURE>
</PARA>
</SECTION>
<SECTION SCID = "T060004A234S"><SCTITLE/>

<PARA INFOID = "T060004A234" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "12Sep2003"     INFOTYPE = "PARTS" INDSW = "ON">
<TITLE>Parking Brake</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>A built-in type parking brake with built-in calipers has been adopted.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>A stock type center lever parking brake lever has been adopted.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740188c" WIDTH = "6.903in" HEIGHT = "3.153in" />
</FIGURE>

<MECA ID = "T060004A234S019" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "26Nov2004"     MECATYPE = "GENERAL" INDSW = "OFF">
<TITLE>Built-in Caliper Construction</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>The parking brake mechanism consists of a crank lever that transmits the operating force of the parking brake lever to the actuator assembly, an actuator assembly that pushes out the adjusting bolt by rotation of the crank lever, and a sleeve nut that transmits the force of the adjusting bolt to the piston.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>The adjuster mechanism consists of the adjusting bolt and sleeve nut of the actuator assembly, which adjust the screw-in amount according to the protrusion of the piston, and a bearing is provided to allow the sleeve nut to rotate smoothly.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740157c" WIDTH = "6.903in" HEIGHT = "3.361in" />
</FIGURE>
</MECA>

<MECA ID = "T060004A234S017" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "21Oct2004"     MECATYPE = "ACTUATE" INDSW = "OFF">
<TITLE>Built-in Caliper Operation</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>When the parking brake lever is operated, the crank lever rotates via the parking brake cable.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>The crank lever is connected to the input shaft of the actuator assembly; when the crank lever rotates, the check balls in the actuator assembly slide the adjusting bolt in the push-out direction due to the change in groove depth.</PTXT></ITEM>
<ITEM><PTXT>When the adjusting bolt slides, the brake pads are pressed against the disc via the sleeve nut and piston, generating braking force.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740158c" WIDTH = "6.917in" HEIGHT = "3.569in" />
</FIGURE>
</MECA>

<MECA ID = "T060004A234S018" UPDATE = "ORIGINAL" LDUP = "21Oct2004"     MECATYPE = "ACTUATE" INDSW = "OFF">
<TITLE>Built-in Caliper Adjuster Operation</TITLE>
<LIST1 TYPE = "UNORDERED"><ITEM><PTXT>When the brake pads wear and the clearance between the pads and rotor exceeds a certain value, the hydraulic pressure from pedal operation causes the sleeve nut to advance while rotating, adjusting the clearance. In addition, the piston and sleeve nut are engaged at the clutch surface; when the hydraulic pressure from pedal operation reaches a certain value, the sleeve nut stops rotating and adjustment stops, preventing over-adjustment and keeping the clearance between the pads and rotor appropriate.</PTXT></ITEM></LIST1>
<FIGURE>
<GRAPHIC GRAPHICNAME = "a4740159c" WIDTH = "6.917in" HEIGHT = "3.764in" />
</FIGURE>
</MECA>
</PARA>
</SECTION>
